document "Minecraft Guide"

description "A complete beginner's guide to Minecraft Bedrock Edition."

topic "How To Play"

    description "Learn the basics of Minecraft Bedrock Edition, from creating your first world to surviving, exploring, building, and progressing."

    section "What Is Minecraft?"

        text
            Minecraft is a sandbox game where you can explore, gather resources, build structures, craft items, fight mobs, and create your own goals.

            Minecraft does not have one required way to play. You can build houses and cities, explore the world, create farms, experiment with redstone, fight bosses, or simply explore.
        end

        note
            This guide is written for Minecraft Bedrock Edition.
        end

    end

    section "Game Modes"

        subsection "Survival"

            text
                Survival mode is the main progression-based way to play Minecraft.

                You begin with limited resources and must gather materials, craft tools, find food, build shelter, and protect yourself from hostile mobs.

                You have health and hunger, and most resources must be obtained through gameplay.
            end

        end

        subsection "Creative"

            text
                Creative mode gives you access to Minecraft's blocks and items without requiring normal resource gathering.

                You can fly, build freely, and use commands and other tools to create large projects quickly.
            end

        end

        subsection "Adventure"

            text
                Adventure mode is designed mainly for custom maps and experiences.

                Your ability to interact with blocks can be restricted, allowing map creators to control how players interact with the world.
            end

        end

        subsection "Spectator"

            text
                Spectator mode allows you to observe the world without normally interacting with it.

                You can move freely through the world and observe players, mobs, and other entities.
            end

        end

    end

    section "Creating A World"

        text
            When creating a world, you can choose settings such as the game mode, difficulty, world seed, multiplayer options, and world-generation settings.

            If you are learning Minecraft for the first time, Survival on Normal difficulty provides a good introduction to the game's progression.
        end

        subsection "World Name"

            text
                Give your world a name that makes it easy to identify. You can change the name later.
            end

        end

        subsection "Game Mode"

            text
                Choose Survival if you want normal resource gathering and progression.

                Choose Creative if you want to freely build and experiment without normal Survival restrictions.
            end

        end

        subsection "Difficulty"

            text
                Difficulty affects hostile mobs, combat, hunger, and other parts of Survival gameplay.

                Peaceful removes most hostile mobs and prevents hunger from reducing your health.

                Easy, Normal, and Hard progressively increase the challenge.
            end

        end

        tip
            You can create a separate Creative world for testing builds, redstone contraptions, commands, or ideas before using them in Survival.
        end

    end

    section "Controls"

        text
            Minecraft Bedrock Edition supports different control methods depending on the device you are using.

            For complete button mappings and touch controls, see the Controls guide.
        end

        link "Controls Guide" "Controls.html"

    end

    section "The First Few Minutes"

        subsection "Gather Wood"

            text
                One of the first resources you should collect is wood.

                Find a tree and break some logs. Logs can be converted into planks, which are used in many early crafting recipes.
            end

            list
                Find a tree
                Break several logs
                Open your inventory
                Convert logs into planks
            end

        end

        subsection "Make A Crafting Table"

            text
                A crafting table provides access to the 3 × 3 crafting grid used for many Minecraft recipes.

                Craft one early because many useful tools and items require it.
            end

            list
                Convert logs into planks
                Use four planks to craft a crafting table
                Place the crafting table somewhere accessible
            end

        end

        subsection "Craft Basic Tools"

            text
                Your first tools will usually be made from wood.

                A wooden pickaxe allows you to mine stone and obtain cobblestone. Once you have cobblestone, you can upgrade to stone tools.
            end

            list
                Craft a wooden pickaxe
                Mine stone
                Collect cobblestone
                Craft a stone pickaxe
                Craft a stone axe
                Craft a stone shovel
                Craft a stone sword
            end

        end

        subsection "Find Food"

            text
                Food restores hunger and is an important part of surviving.

                Early in the game, you can obtain food from animals, crops, and other sources.
            end

            note
                Different foods restore different amounts of hunger and saturation.
            end

        end

    end

    section "Your First Shelter"

        text
            A shelter gives you a safe place to spend the night and store your early resources.

            It does not need to be large or complicated. A simple enclosed room is enough when you are starting.
        end

        subsection "Choose A Location"

            text
                Look for a reasonably flat area with useful resources nearby.

                Being close to trees, water, food, and exposed stone can make the beginning of a world easier.
            end

        end

        subsection "Build The Shelter"

            list
                Choose a location
                Build walls
                Add a roof
                Add an entrance
                Place light sources
                Place a crafting table
                Place storage
                Add a bed when you have the required materials
            end

        end

        tip
            Your first shelter is temporary. You can replace it later with a larger and more detailed base.
        end

    end

    section "Surviving The Night"

        text
            Hostile mobs can spawn in dark areas and become a major threat when you are starting.

            Staying inside a properly enclosed and lit shelter is one of the simplest ways to survive your first night.
        end

        warning
            Make sure your shelter is actually enclosed and properly lit. A small room is not automatically safe.
        end

        list
            Return to your shelter before night if possible
            Close the entrance
            Light the interior
            Keep valuable items stored safely
            Avoid unnecessary fights while poorly equipped
        end

    end

    section "Mining"

        text
            Mining is one of the main ways to obtain useful resources.

            Stone provides basic materials, while deeper areas of the world contain ores and other resources.

            Bring appropriate tools and be prepared to manage your inventory while mining.
        end

        subsection "Useful Early Resources"

            list
                Coal
                Iron
                Copper
                Redstone
                Gold
                Diamonds
                Lapis Lazuli
            end

        end

        subsection "Mining Safety"

            warning
                Underground areas can contain hostile mobs, lava, and other hazards.
            end

            list
                Bring food
                Bring extra tools
                Bring light sources
                Keep spare blocks available
                Avoid digging blindly into unknown areas
                Keep track of where you came from
            end

        end

    end

    section "Crafting"

        text
            Crafting allows you to turn collected materials into tools, equipment, building materials, food, and many other items.

            You can craft some items directly from your inventory. More complicated recipes generally require a crafting table or another specialized workstation.
        end

        tip
            If you do not know how to craft something, use the recipe book to find available recipes and their required materials.
        end

    end

    section "Tools"

        text
            Tools allow you to collect resources more efficiently.

            Different tools are designed for different types of blocks and tasks.
        end

        list
            Pickaxe — Mining many stone-based blocks and ores
            Axe — Chopping wood and related blocks
            Shovel — Digging dirt, sand, gravel, and similar blocks
            Hoe — Farming and harvesting certain crops
        end

        note
            Using an appropriate tool usually makes gathering resources faster and can be required for obtaining certain blocks or resources.
        end

    end

    section "Food And Hunger"

        text
            In Survival, your hunger bar affects your ability to survive and recover.

            Keep a reliable supply of food with you when exploring, mining, or traveling long distances.
        end

        subsection "Early Food Sources"

            list
                Animals
                Crops
                Fishing
                Naturally generated food
            end

        end

        tip
            Establishing a renewable food source early makes long mining and exploration trips much easier.
        end

    end

    section "Armor"

        text
            Armor reduces the damage you take from many sources.

            Early armor can make exploring caves and fighting hostile mobs significantly safer.
        end

        list
            Helmet
            Chestplate
            Leggings
            Boots
        end

    end

    section "Exploring"

        text
            Minecraft's world contains many different biomes, structures, resources, and points of interest.

            Exploration can provide materials and discoveries that are difficult to find near your starting location.
        end

        subsection "Bring These Items"

            list
                Food
                Tools
                Weapons
                Armor
                Light sources
                Building blocks
                Empty inventory space
            end

        end

        tip
            Record the coordinates of important locations so you can find them again.
        end

    end

    section "Coordinates"

        text
            Coordinates tell you where you are in the Minecraft world.

            The three coordinate values represent your position along the X, Y, and Z axes.

            X generally represents east and west, Y represents vertical height, and Z generally represents north and south.
        end

        subsection "Using Coordinates"

            text
                Coordinates are useful for finding your base, villages, structures, mines, farms, portals, and other important locations.

                Write down or otherwise record important coordinates when exploring.
            end

        end

    end

    section "Inventory Management"

        text
            Your inventory has limited space, so organizing your items makes exploration and building easier.

            Keep frequently used items in predictable locations and store extra materials at your base.
        end

        list
            Keep tools together
            Keep food easily accessible
            Store building materials separately
            Store valuable resources safely
            Empty your inventory before long mining trips
        end

    end

    section "Building"

        text
            Building is one of Minecraft's biggest features.

            You can start with simple houses and gradually learn more advanced techniques such as symmetry, depth, roofs, towers, walls, landscaping, and large-scale planning.
        end

        subsection "Basic Building Principles"

            list
                Plan the size before building
                Create a clear shape
                Add depth to flat walls
                Use multiple materials
                Add windows and entrances
                Add lighting
                Add interior details
                Landscape the surrounding area
            end

        end

        tip
            Large builds are easier when you plan their dimensions before placing hundreds or thousands of blocks.
        end

    end

    section "Storage"

        text
            Storage allows you to organize the resources you collect.

            Chests are one of the first storage blocks you will use. As your world grows, you can build larger storage rooms and automated sorting systems.
        end

        list
            Craft chests
            Place storage near your main crafting area
            Group similar items together
            Keep valuable resources organized
            Label or otherwise identify large storage areas
        end

    end

    section "Redstone"

        text
            Redstone is Minecraft's system for creating mechanisms and automated machines.

            It can be used for doors, lights, farms, sorting systems, traps, transportation systems, and much more.
        end

        list
            Redstone Dust
            Redstone Torch
            Lever
            Button
            Pressure Plate
            Repeater
            Comparator
            Observer
            Piston
            Hopper
        end

        tip
            Start with simple redstone circuits before attempting complicated machines.
        end

        link "Redstone Guide" "Redstone.html"

    end

    section "Farms"

        text
            Farms allow you to repeatedly produce resources with less manual work.

            Farms can be completely manual, partially automated, or highly automated using redstone and Minecraft mechanics.
        end

        list
            Crop farms
            Animal farms
            Tree farms
            Mob farms
            Iron farms
            Experience farms
            Food farms
        end

        link "Farms Guide" "Farms.html"

    end

    section "Villages"

        text
            Villages contain villagers, useful structures, crops, and other resources.

            Villagers can obtain professions and offer trades that can become valuable as your world progresses.
        end

        tip
            If you find a useful village, consider protecting it and recording its coordinates.
        end

        link "Villagers Guide" "Villagers.html"

    end

    section "Enchanting"

        text
            Enchanting allows you to add special abilities to equipment.

            Enchantments can improve tools, weapons, armor, and other equipment.
        end

        note
            Enchanting is an advanced progression system and normally becomes more useful after you have established a reliable supply of resources and experience.
        end

        link "Enchanting Guide" "Enchanting.html"

    end

    section "The Nether"

        text
            The Nether is a dangerous dimension containing unique biomes, structures, mobs, and resources.

            Nether travel can also be extremely useful because distances traveled through the Nether correspond to much larger distances in the Overworld.
        end

        warning
            Prepare carefully before entering the Nether. Losing your equipment there can make recovery difficult.
        end

        subsection "Before Entering"

            list
                Bring food
                Bring armor
                Bring weapons
                Bring tools
                Bring building blocks
                Bring light sources
                Know the location of your Nether portal
                Have a plan for returning to the Overworld
            end

        end

        link "Dimensions Guide" "Dimensions.html"

    end

    section "The End"

        text
            The End is a separate dimension containing the Ender Dragon and End Cities.

            Reaching the End requires substantial preparation compared with the early stages of Survival.
        end

        note
            Defeating the Ender Dragon is a major milestone, but it does not mean that your Minecraft world is finished.
        end

        link "Dimensions Guide" "Dimensions.html"

    end

    section "Progression"

        text
            Minecraft has a natural progression, although you are free to ignore it.

            A typical Survival progression starts with wood and stone, moves into iron equipment, and eventually reaches stronger equipment and advanced systems.
        end

        list
            Gather wood
            Make basic tools
            Obtain stone
            Find food
            Establish shelter
            Obtain iron
            Upgrade equipment
            Explore the world
            Build farms
            Learn enchanting
            Trade with villagers
            Explore the Nether
            Prepare for the End
            Defeat the Ender Dragon
            Continue building and exploring
        end

    end

    section "Death"

        text
            If your health reaches zero, you die and normally respawn at your bed or another valid respawn location.

            Your dropped items can usually be recovered if you return to where you died before they disappear.
        end

        warning
            Keep important resources stored safely before taking unnecessary risks.
        end

        tip
            Recording the coordinates of your base makes recovering after death much easier.
        end

    end

    section "Saving And Leaving"

        text
            Minecraft automatically saves your world while you play.

            When you are finished playing, use the normal game menu to leave the world rather than abruptly closing the game whenever possible.
        end

        note
            Your worlds are stored separately, so changes made in one world do not normally affect another world.
        end

    end

    section "What To Do Next"

        text
            Once you understand the basics, Minecraft becomes much more open-ended.

            You can focus on whichever parts of the game you enjoy most.
        end

        list
            Build a larger base
            Start a storage system
            Create farms
            Learn redstone
            Explore new biomes
            Find structures
            Improve your equipment
            Learn enchanting
            Trade with villagers
            Explore the Nether
            Visit the End
            Build large projects
            Create your own goals
        end

        tip
            There is no single correct way to play Minecraft. The best next project is the one you actually want to build, explore, or learn.
        end

    end

end